WEBVTT

1
00:00:40.875 --> 00:00:45.166
<b>                </b>

2
00:00:45.166 --> 00:00:49.041
<b>On the battlefield, after hearing all of</b>
<b>Duryodhana's words,</b>

3
00:00:49.041 --> 00:00:50.750
<b>Sanjaya describes </b>

4
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.083
<b> how</b>

5
00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:54.583
<b>Grandsire Bhishma, without uttering a word, </b>

6
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:56.208
<b>lifted his conch</b>

7
00:00:56.208 --> 00:01:00.666
<b>and blew it with a roar that resembled thunder. </b>

8
00:01:00.666 --> 00:01:02.708
<b>Upon hearing this,</b>
<b>Duryodhana's heart </b>

9
00:01:02.708 --> 00:01:04.750
<b>swelled with immense joy. </b>

10
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:05.750
<b>Thereafter, </b>

11
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.333
<b>in the Kaurava camp, </b>

12
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:08.291
<b>conches,</b>

13
00:01:08.291 --> 00:01:09.208
<b>kettledrums, </b>

14
00:01:09.208 --> 00:01:10.041
<b>tabors, </b>

15
00:01:10.041 --> 00:01:10.875
<b>drums, </b>

16
00:01:10.875 --> 00:01:12.000
<b>and horns</b>

17
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.000
<b> all began to sound together, </b>

18
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.375
<b>creating a tumultuous noise.</b>

19
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.250


20
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.750
<b>Duryodhana's words</b>

21
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.083
<b>were an attempt to encourage</b>
<b>Grandsire Bhishma, </b>

22
00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:30.333
<b>upon whom he relied. </b>

23
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:37.500
<b>Not only did he mention Bhishma's prowess</b>
<b>in battle and his invincibility before Guru Drona,</b>

24
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.208
<b>but</b>

25
00:01:38.208 --> 00:01:45.291
<b>he also instructed all the warriors to</b>
<b>protect Bhishma from their respective positions. </b>

26
00:01:45.291 --> 00:01:50.583
<b>In response, Grandsire Bhishma</b>
<b>simply blew his conch. </b>

27
00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:53.625
<b>This act brought Duryodhana great pleasure, </b>

28
00:01:53.625 --> 00:01:54.541
<b>yet </b>

29
00:01:54.541 --> 00:02:01.333
<b>he failed to understand the reason behind</b>
<b>Bhishma's decision to forgo words</b>

30
00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:02.166
<b>and </b>

31
00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:04.250
<b>directly sound the conch. </b>

32
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.791
<b>Duryodhana sees only what he wants to see. </b>

33
00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:11.375
<b>Grandsire Bhishma also knows</b>
<b>that Duryodhana's </b>

34
00:02:11.375 --> 00:02:17.041
<b>words lack genuine love or respect for anyone. </b>

35
00:02:17.041 --> 00:02:18.541
<b>If anything is concealed, </b>

36
00:02:18.541 --> 00:02:22.125
<b>it is only his desire to win </b>

37
00:02:22.125 --> 00:02:24.875
<b>and his eagerness for this war. </b>

38
00:02:24.875 --> 00:02:27.666
<b>In such a situation, why waste words? </b>

39
00:02:27.666 --> 00:02:30.000
<b>If he wants to hear a declaration of war, </b>

40
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.833
<b>then let the conch suffice. </b>

41
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:32.958
<b>After all, </b>

42
00:02:32.958 --> 00:02:38.291
<b>until now, all of Grandsire's words</b>
<b>and efforts have been in vain. </b>

43
00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:40.583
<b>He tried many times </b>

44
00:02:40.583 --> 00:02:42.625
<b> to persuade Duryodhana to mend his ways, </b>

45
00:02:42.625 --> 00:02:44.583
<b>follow the right path,</b>

46
00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:48.750
<b>and not commit such adharma</b>
<b>that would ultimately lead to his destruction. </b>

47
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.458
<b>But what was the benefit? </b>

48
00:02:50.458 --> 00:02:52.583
<b>And now, the war has already arrived.</b>

49
00:02:52.583 --> 00:02:54.666
<b>What is the point of talking now? </b>

50
00:02:54.666 --> 00:02:57.958
<b>Bhishma will now simply fulfill his duty. </b>

51
00:02:57.958 --> 00:03:02.625
<b>With all his ability, he will keep his</b>
<b>promise to Hastinapura. </b>

52
00:03:02.625 --> 00:03:03.916
<b>Victory, however, </b>

53
00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:06.458
<b>will be determined by Lord Krishna alone. </b>

54
00:03:06.458 --> 00:03:10.458
<b>This, Grandsire knows in his heart as well.</b>

55
00:03:11.125 --> 00:03:15.333


56
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:19.416
<b>No matter how much praise Duryodhana</b>

57
00:03:19.416 --> 00:03:24.291
<b>showers upon Grandsire Bhishma</b>
<b>or how sweetly he expresses it, </b>

58
00:03:24.291 --> 00:03:29.916
<b>it will have no effect on Grandsire Bhishma. </b>

59
00:03:29.916 --> 00:03:31.708
<b>Right or wrong,  </b>

60
00:03:31.708 --> 00:03:34.291
<b>whatever Bhishma is doing, </b>

61
00:03:34.291 --> 00:03:38.166
<b>he is doing it for his own conscience. </b>

62
00:03:38.166 --> 00:03:40.541
<b>His conscience tells him </b>

63
00:03:40.541 --> 00:03:45.541
<b>that the value of his given word is</b>
<b>greater than life itself, </b>

64
00:03:45.541 --> 00:03:47.208
<b>so he will fight</b>

65
00:03:47.208 --> 00:03:49.000
<b>for Hastinapura </b>

66
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.791
<b>and the Kauravas. </b>

67
00:03:51.791 --> 00:03:55.750
<b>Now, whether someone praises </b>

68
00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:57.750
<b>or criticizes him, </b>

69
00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:01.958
<b>it holds no significance. </b>

70
00:04:01.958 --> 00:04:04.500
<b>Good or bad,</b>

71
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.666
<b>do not be influenced by anyone's words. </b>

72
00:04:08.666 --> 00:04:15.041
<b>And if you find yourself easily swayed, </b>

73
00:04:15.041 --> 00:04:16.958
<b>it is a sign </b>

74
00:04:16.958 --> 00:04:22.291
<b>to strive for self-control. </b>

75
00:04:22.291 --> 00:04:25.625
<b>Let me explain it this way:</b>

76
00:04:25.625 --> 00:04:29.916
<b>whether someone throws a stone</b>
<b>into a flowing river </b>

77
00:04:29.916 --> 00:04:34.458
<b>or quenches their thirst with its pristine water, </b>

78
00:04:34.458 --> 00:04:39.583
<b>the river's purpose is to keep</b>
<b>flowing towards its direction.</b>

79
00:04:39.583 --> 00:04:40.875
<b>You, too,</b>

80
00:04:40.875 --> 00:04:44.583
<b>should continue steadfastly towards your goal, </b>

81
00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:47.208
<b>no matter how much commotion </b>

82
00:04:47.208 --> 00:04:49.541
<b>or noise surrounds you.</b>

83
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.875


84
00:04:54.875 --> 00:04:57.208
<b>Bhishma is helpless. </b>

85
00:04:57.208 --> 00:05:04.708
<b>Even though he does not desire this war,</b>
<b>he must fight it. </b>

86
00:05:04.708 --> 00:05:13.625
<b>He has given his word to work for the benefit of</b>

87
00:05:13.625 --> 00:05:16.291
<b>It is essential to understand the context here.</b>

88
00:05:16.291 --> 00:05:18.416
<b>These words </b>

89
00:05:18.416 --> 00:05:22.583
<b>and promises are bound by the historical time </b>

90
00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:25.500
<b>when the value of words in society </b>

91
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.666
<b>was considered greater than life itself. </b>

92
00:05:29.666 --> 00:05:32.958
<b>It is because of this that Bhishma's destiny </b>

93
00:05:32.958 --> 00:05:35.041
<b>is written to fight this war, </b>

94
00:05:35.041 --> 00:05:36.750
<b>Then he will fight it,</b>

95
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:41.791
<b>and if it is written for him to endure  </b>

96
00:05:41.791 --> 00:05:43.583
<b>You, too, </b>

97
00:05:43.583 --> 00:05:47.958
<b>may sometimes feel helpless. </b>

98
00:05:47.958 --> 00:05:50.166
<b>What will you do then? </b>

99
00:05:50.166 --> 00:05:56.791
<b>Should Bhishma break his promise? </b>

100
00:05:56.791 --> 00:06:02.583
<b>Is that right? </b>

101
00:06:02.583 --> 00:06:04.250
<b>In today's context, </b>

102
00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:08.125
<b>let us try to understand it this way: </b>

103
00:06:08.125 --> 00:06:12.083
<b>every word has two meanings behind it.</b>

104
00:06:12.083 --> 00:06:14.791
<b>One is its literal meaning, </b>

105
00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:16.916
<b>and the other </b>

106
00:06:16.916 --> 00:06:19.708
<b>is its implied meaning. </b>

107
00:06:19.708 --> 00:06:21.333
<b>The implied meaning </b>

108
00:06:21.333 --> 00:06:24.708
<b>is more important. </b>

109
00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:26.166
<b>For example, </b>

110
00:06:26.166 --> 00:06:28.375
<b>take these words: </b>

111
00:06:28.375 --> 00:06:31.875
<b>"I will never punish you." </b>

112
00:06:31.875 --> 00:06:35.041
<b>The literal meaning here </b>

113
00:06:35.041 --> 00:06:37.166
<b>is that no matter what happens, </b>

114
00:06:37.166 --> 00:06:40.958
<b>whether it is for your benefit or detriment, </b>

115
00:06:40.958 --> 00:06:43.958
<b>but under any circumstance, </b>

116
00:06:43.958 --> 00:06:47.791
<b>I will never, punish you. </b>

117
00:06:47.791 --> 00:06:49.500
<b>But  </b>

118
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.500
<b>what is the implied meaning? </b>

119
00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.333
<b>The implied meaning could be </b>

120
00:06:56.333 --> 00:06:58.541
<b>that I love you, </b>

121
00:06:58.541 --> 00:07:02.791
<b>so why would I ever punish you? </b>

122
00:07:02.791 --> 00:07:04.125
<b>However, </b>

123
00:07:04.125 --> 00:07:06.333
<b>if someone starts walking down the wrong path </b>

124
00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:11.791
<b>and it becomes necessary to shake them up</b>
<b>for their own good, </b>

125
00:07:11.791 --> 00:07:15.125
<b>to punish them, </b>

126
00:07:15.125 --> 00:07:17.708
<b>then wouldn't it be right </b>

127
00:07:17.708 --> 00:07:24.125
<b>to do what aligns with the implied</b>
<b>meaning of love and welfare,</b>

128
00:07:24.125 --> 00:07:29.208
<b> even if it means punishment? </b>

129
00:07:29.208 --> 00:07:30.583
<b>The meanings of feelings </b>

130
00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:32.625
<b>are greater than the meanings of words.</b>

131
00:07:32.625 --> 00:07:36.000
<b>Know this.</b>

132
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.000
<b> In a social</b>

133
00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:41.708
<b>or a family setting, never fall into the regret </b>

134
00:07:41.708 --> 00:07:45.208
<b>of what verbal words you spoke. </b>

135
00:07:45.208 --> 00:07:49.541
<b>The context of what you said is important; </b>

136
00:07:49.541 --> 00:07:53.333
<b>its implied meaning is important.</b>

137
00:07:53.333 --> 00:08:00.333
<b>Do not do something unrighteous out of the guilt</b>
<b>of a promise made to someone.</b>

138
00:08:00.875 --> 00:08:05.083


139
00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:07.500
<b>Grandsire Bhishma </b>

140
00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.500
<b>is only thinking about duty. </b>

141
00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:15.458
<b>He is not considering</b>
<b>Duryodhana's diplomatic words </b>

142
00:08:15.458 --> 00:08:18.333
<b>or his praise. </b>

143
00:08:18.333 --> 00:08:21.708
<b>Even if Duryodhana continues to believe</b>

144
00:08:21.708 --> 00:08:27.625
<b>that Bhishma is being encouraged by his words, </b>

145
00:08:27.625 --> 00:08:30.708
<b>what difference does it make? </b>

146
00:08:30.708 --> 00:08:33.833
<b>When the goal becomes paramount, </b>

147
00:08:33.833 --> 00:08:38.708
<b>such small matters lose their significance. </b>

148
00:08:38.708 --> 00:08:41.125
<b>When a great archer </b>

149
00:08:41.125 --> 00:08:44.125
<b>aims at a target, </b>

150
00:08:44.125 --> 00:08:48.375
<b>he sees only the target; </b>

151
00:08:48.375 --> 00:08:54.833
<b>he does not see any other scenes</b>
<b>unfolding around him. </b>

152
00:08:54.833 --> 00:08:56.583
<b>In professional life, </b>

153
00:08:56.583 --> 00:09:00.291
<b>keep your focus on your goal. </b>

154
00:09:00.291 --> 00:09:02.583
<b>Diplomacy and deceit </b>

155
00:09:02.583 --> 00:09:06.291
<b>are the actions of insecure people. </b>

156
00:09:06.291 --> 00:09:07.791
<b>They will continue to exist. </b>

157
00:09:07.791 --> 00:09:16.375
<b>Whereas, praise and criticism</b>
<b>are also a part of life. </b>

158
00:09:16.375 --> 00:09:18.750
<b>What is important </b>

159
00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:23.250
<b>is that you do not get distracted</b>
<b>from your path by all of this. </b>

160
00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:26.166
<b>You just keep moving forward, </b>

161
00:09:26.166 --> 00:09:28.458
<b>towards your true direction.</b>

